Test: com.google.javascript.jscomp.IntegrationTest.testIssue772()
Method Code:
457 :     private void fixTypeNode(Node typeNode) {
458 :       if (typeNode.isString()) {
459 :         String name = typeNode.getString();
460 :         int endIndex = name.indexOf('.');
461 :         if (endIndex == -1) {
462 :           endIndex = name.length();
463 :         }
464 :         String baseName = name.substring(0, endIndex);
465 :         Var aliasVar = aliases.get(baseName);
466 :         if (aliasVar != null) {
467 :           Node aliasedNode = aliasVar.getInitialValue();
468 :           aliasUsages.add(new AliasedTypeNode(typeNode, aliasedNode.getQualifiedName() + name.substring(endIndex)));
469 :         }
470 :       }
471 : 
472 :       for (Node child = typeNode.getFirstChild(); child != null;
473 :            child = child.getNext()) {
474 :         fixTypeNode(child);
475 :       }
476 :     }
Hypothesis: The method performs a single-pass alias substitution on type strings and does not recursively resolve transitive aliases in JSDoc type references. As a result, when an alias expands to a qualified name rooted at another alias, the rewritten type remains partially aliased and later type parsing fails.

